<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milindapanha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Questions of King Milinda, Milinda Pañha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Milindapanha, or Questions of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Milinda</a>, is a Buddhist text in Pali, probably compiled between the 1st century BCE and the 2nd century CE. It records a dialogue in which the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a> answers the questions of King Milinda, identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> king Menander I, on rebirth, the self and other points of doctrine. It is highly regarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milindapanha.jpg" alt="Milindapanha" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folios_of_the_Bhikkhuni-patimokkha_(BL_IO_Man.Pali_21).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Milindapañhā, whose title means Questions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Milinda</a>, dates from sometime between 100 BCE and 200 CE. It purports to record a dialogue between the Buddhist sage Nāgasena and the 2nd-century BCE Indo-Greek king Menander I, called Milinda in Pali. Scholars generally accept that the work is composite, with additions made over time, and note that the Chinese versions are substantially shorter. The text may first have been written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and Oskar von Hinüber suggests, from a Chinese translation and some unique concepts in the text, that the original language might have been Gandhari.</p><p>Apart from the Sri Lankan Pali edition and its derivatives no other copies are known. The oldest Pali manuscript was copied in 1495, and significant sections are lost, making it the only Pali text known to have been passed down incomplete.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work begins with a background history, then turns to questions on distinguishing characteristics, such as attention, wisdom, feeling and perception, and questions for the cutting off of perplexity, which take up transmigration and rebirth and the soul. It goes on to eighty-two dilemmas, a question solved by inference, the special qualities of asceticism, and questions on similes.</p><p>In the text Menander is the king of the city of Sāgala, learned, eloquent and wise, accompanied by a guard of 500 Greek soldiers, with counsellors named Demetrius and Antiochus. Buddhist tradition relates that after his discussions with Nāgasena he adopted the Buddhist faith for as long as life should last, gave his kingdom to his son and later attained enlightenment. Von Hinüber observes that Menander is a historical figure but Nāgasena is otherwise unknown, that the text includes anachronisms, and that the dialogue shows no sign of Greek influence and can be traced to the Upanisads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Milindapañhā is regarded as canonical in Burmese Buddhism, as part of the Khuddaka Nikāya, but not in Thai or Sri Lankan Buddhism. An abridged version appears in Chinese translations, and the Monk Nāgasena Sutra, translated in the Eastern Jin dynasty, corresponds to its first three chapters. The Grande Inscription d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, engraved in 1701 on the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a>, mentions it. Thomas Rhys Davids called it the masterpiece of Indian prose, though Moriz Winternitz held that this is true only of the earlier parts. It has been translated into English by Rhys Davids in 1890, Isaline Blew Horner in 1969 and Maria Heim in 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milinda Panha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indo-Greek king Menander, who ruled in the second century BCE, is remembered in a Buddhist text, the <b>Questions of King Milinda</b>, as a ruler who debated with the monk Nagasena and ended as his disciple.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
